{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said to Moses and to Aaron.</b> Even though Scripture mentions them both for the utterance, Moses was the principal. And likewise it is written (Exodus 20): ״כבד את אביך ואת אמך״ — and the father is the principal. And because Aaron toiled in the signs along with Moses, therefore Scripture included him." "<b>In the land of Egypt.</b> Outside the metropolis, because it was full of idols; for Scripture did not say 'in the city of Egypt.' And so Moses mentioned, 'as I go out of the city,' and did not pray in the city. And the fact that the Shekhinah rested in the land of Egypt — our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded it to teach you: before the land of Israel was chosen, all lands were fit for divine speech (dibbur); once the land of Israel was chosen, all lands were disqualified for divine speech. Before Jerusalem was chosen, all the land of Israel was fit for divine speech; once Jerusalem was chosen, all the land of Israel was disqualified for divine speech. Before the eternal House was chosen, Jerusalem was fit for divine speech; once the eternal House was chosen, Jerusalem was excluded — as it is written (Psalms 132:14): ״זאת מנוחתי עדי עד״. Before Aaron was chosen, all Israel were fit for the priesthood; once Aaron was chosen, all Israel were excluded. Before David was chosen, all Israel were fit for kingship, as it is said (Exodus 19:6): ״ואתם תהיו לי ממלכת כהנים״ and so on; once David was chosen, all Israel were excluded. And even outside the land, divine speech would not adhere except in a pure place or upon a river, as it is said (Daniel 8:2): ״ואני הייתי על אובל אולי״, and it is written (Daniel 10): ״ואני הייתי על יד הנהר הגדול הוא חדקל״, and it is written (Ezekiel 1:1): ״ואני בתוך הגולה על נהר כבר״." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> Resh Lakish said: How great is the belovedness of Israel, that the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself to them in a place of idolatry and in a place of impurity, in order to redeem them. To what may the matter be compared? To a priest whose terumah fell into a cemetery. He said: What shall I do? To defile myself — impossible. To abandon my terumah — impossible. It is better that I defile myself for one hour, and then I return and purify myself, rather than abandon my terumah. So too Israel are the terumah of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Jeremiah 2:3): ״קודש ישראל לה׳ ראשית תבואתו״. They were in a cemetery, as it is said: 'for there is no house in which there is not a dead person,' and it is written (Numbers 33:3–4): ״ומצרים מקברים את אשר הכה ה׳ בהם כל בכור״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: How shall I redeem them? To abandon them — impossible. It is better, as it were, that I descend to rescue them, as it is said (Exodus 3:8): ״וארד להצילו מיד מצרים״. When He brought them out, He called Aaron and said to him: 'Purify Me,' as it is said (Leviticus 16:33): ״וכפר את מקדש הקדש וכפר על הקדש״ — end."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "Prooftext citations given as book name only or book+chapter only have been normalized to include the verse number where the Hebrew quotation uniquely identifies it.", 2 "The citation 'כי אין בית אשר אין שם מת' is given without a Scripture reference; rendered as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:01.879501511Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}